# Copy this to `.seed`, replace the placeholders, and chmod 600 it.
#
#     cp harness/bns/.seed.example harness/bns/.seed
#     chmod 600 harness/bns/.seed
#
# `.seed` is gitignored and `.seed.example` is not, so NEVER put a real phrase
# in this file. There is a test that fails the build if anything here validates
# as a BIP39 phrase, which is why the placeholders below are deliberately not
# real words — they fail the checksum on purpose.
#
# The tool refuses to read `.seed` at all if its permissions let anyone else on
# the machine read it. It never prints a phrase, never logs one, never accepts
# one as a command-line argument, and never sends one anywhere. Shell history is
# a file too, and it is not mode 600.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE SEED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One BIP39 phrase on one line. Twelve or twenty-four words, lowercase,
# single-spaced. Nothing else needed.

placeholderone placeholdertwo placeholderthree placeholderfour placeholderfive placeholdersix placeholderseven placeholdereight placeholdernine placeholderten placeholdereleven placeholdertwelve

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEVERAL SEEDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One phrase per line, and the tool walks them in order: it derives every
# account from every seed, matches them all against your name list at once, and
# reports which seed reached what.
#
# LABEL THEM. A label is anything before the first colon, and it is the only
# thing that makes a multi-seed report actionable — "twelve names unreachable"
# is a fact you cannot do anything with, while "unreachable, and the ledger seed
# was not in the file" tells you what to fetch next. Labels are printed; phrases
# are not.
#
#   old-hiro-2019:  word word word …
#   ledger-backup:  word word word …
#   phone-wallet:   word word word …
#
# An unlabelled line still works and is reported as `seed-2`, `seed-3` and so
# on — counted by position, so a phrase is never identified by its own words.
#
# A line whose checksum fails stops the whole run, by line number, without
# echoing the line. That is deliberate: a mistyped word derives a perfectly
# valid set of addresses that hold nothing, so continuing would report your
# names as unreachable when the truth is one word is wrong. Being told "line 4
# is not a valid phrase" is recoverable. Being told "you own nothing" is not.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.

# old-hiro-2019: placeholderone placeholdertwo placeholderthree placeholderfour placeholderfive placeholdersix placeholderseven placeholdereight placeholdernine placeholderten placeholdereleven placeholdertwelve
# ledger-backup: placeholderone placeholdertwo placeholderthree placeholderfour placeholderfive placeholdersix placeholderseven placeholdereight placeholdernine placeholderten placeholdereleven placeholdertwelve

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AFTERWARDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Delete `.seed` when the rescue is done. It has served its purpose and it is
# the highest-value file on the machine while it exists.
#
#     rm harness/bns/.seed
